## Open problem

Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Fix a bosonic quantum field theory in $2+1$ spacetime dimensions on closed oriented three-manifolds with a specified tangential structure. Its exact symmetry data include a finite semisimple fusion 2-category $\mathcal C$ of topological codimension-one surface defects: objects are surfaces, 1-morphisms are line junctions, and 2-morphisms are point junctions, with tensor product, unit, associator, interchanger, duals, and coherence maps specified in the semistrict monoidal 2-category convention. Equip these data with a specified dagger structure, positive finite-dimensional Hom-space inner products, and unitary coherence maps. Fix the full background and junction groupoid, the allowed three-dimensional local counterterms, and the relative bulk data. A symmetry topological field theory (SymTFT) is a $3+1$-dimensional topological field theory with a specified boundary whose defect data realize $\mathcal C$. A relative inflow datum is a $3+1$-dimensional bulk theory whose boundary variation cancels the specified background and junction anomaly.

The problem is to define and classify, up to equivalence, the $2$-categorical anomaly-matching structures $\mathcal N\to\mathcal C\to\mathcal S$ and the equivalent SymTFT interfaces. The definition must supply precise injectivity, surjectivity, and exactness conditions for the monoidal 2-functors or their interface replacements. Determine whether the resulting equivalence classes are a complete invariant of the background-transformation phases and global holonomies of $\mathcal C$, modulo the specified local counterterms, together with all compatible relative inflow data. If completeness fails, exhibit a finite semisimple unitary counterexample and identify the missing invariant. No claim that a fusion 2-category by itself specifies a QFT realization is included.

## Open problem references

[Antinucci, Copetti, Gai, and Schäfer-Nameki, *Categorical Anomaly Matching*, §§IV.1, VII, and Appendix A.6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.00982)

[Inamura, Huang, Tiwari, and Schäfer-Nameki, *Lattice Models and Tensor Networks for Gapped Phases with Categorical Symmetry*, Introduction and §2.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.09177)

[Robbins and Roy, *SymTFT Actions, Condensable Algebras and Categorical Anomaly Resolutions*, §§3–5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.05408)

[Balasubramanian, Buican, Delcamp, and Radhakrishnan, *Gauging Non-Invertible Symmetries in (2+1)d Topological Orders*, Introduction, §4, and §7](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.01142)

## Status

The formulation and source status were reviewed on 2026-10-23. Antinucci et al. give tensor-functor/SymTFT anomaly-matching results and finite examples, but state that the higher-categorical theory of anomalous symmetry categories and their interrelations remains in development; their Appendix A.6 places fusion 2-categories in $2+1$d and says classification of the corresponding anomalous symmetry categories is a future direction. Inamura et al. provide a substantial later result: a classification framework for all-boson gapped phases and lattice constructions for non-chiral phases in that class. This does not classify the anomaly-matching structures or their completeness requested here. Robbins and Roy resolve selected categorical examples, while Balasubramanian et al. develop gauging constraints for bosonic topological orders and identify further open extensions.

The earlier research lead placed a fusion 2-category in $3+1$d. It is not adopted here: the reviewed fusion 2-category sources associate that structure with $2+1$d symmetry defects. The 2023 Córdova–Hsin–Zhang work supplies important $3+1$d non-invertible anomaly models, but its stated construction uses specific $4+1$d Abelian two-form gauge theories and does not settle a general categorical classification. The October 2026 Tannaka-duality preprint treats selected $1+1$d fusion-category SPT/fiber-functor cases; its dimension and categorical level differ from this question. These results narrow the current boundary but do not answer the stated general problem.

## Status references

[Córdova, Hsin, and Zhang, *Anomalies of Non-Invertible Symmetries in (3+1)d*, Introduction and §5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.11706)

[Santaniello, Valandro, and Zorzenon, *Non-Invertible SPT Phases from Fusion Rings via Tannaka Duality*, Abstract and §7](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.07165)

