        "physical_scope": "A dimensionless finite-dimensional graded integral used only as an illustration of the Ward identity; it is not identified with the S4 field configuration space and gives no continuum or empirical prediction.",
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "statement": "Let $M=\\mathbb R^2/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2$ be the two-torus with dimensionless angular coordinates $x,y$ modulo $2\\pi$, orientation and volume form $\\mu=dx\\wedge dy$, differential-form algebra $\\Omega^\\bullet(M;\\mathbb C)$, and graded integral $I(F)=\\int_M F_{[2]}$. Let the odd derivation be $Q=d$, set the dimensionless action $S=0$, choose the even insertion $O=1+\\lambda\\,dx\\wedge dy$ for $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and choose the odd deformation $V=\\sin(x)\\,dy$. For every dimensionless real $t$, the integral $Z(t)=I(Oe^{-S-tQV})$ equals $4\\pi^2\\lambda$. This is a finite-dimensional regulator toy integral only; it is not a model of the S4 field configuration space or a continuum physical prediction."
      },
