        "physical_scope": "A finite-dimensional graded integral on a compact manifold with boundary, included only to exhibit the boundary obstruction; it is not an S4 field theory, empirical result, or continuum claim.",
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "statement": "Let $M=[0,1]\\times(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ be the cylinder with dimensionless coordinates $x,y$, orientation and volume form $dx\\wedge dy$, differential-form algebra $\\Omega^\\bullet(M;\\mathbb C)$, and graded integral $I(F)=\\int_M F_{[2]}$. Take the odd derivation $Q=d$, dimensionless action $S=0$, even insertion $O=1$, and odd deformation $V=x\\,dy$. For every dimensionless real $t$, $Z(t)=I(Oe^{-S-tQV})=-2\\pi t$. The change occurs because $I(Q(Ve^{-S-tQV}))=2\\pi\\ne0$: this cycle violates the zero-boundary Q-invariance condition and therefore is not an instance of the finite-dimensional Ward identity."
