## Postulate

Use natural units "'$c='"\\hbar=1"'$. Fix $r>0$ with units of length and the round oriented sphere
$$
'"\\mathbb S"'^3_r='"\\{y\\in\\mathbb R"'^4:'"\\textstyle\\sum_{\\alpha=1}"'^4 y_'"\\alpha"'^2=r''^2'"\\}
"'$$
with its induced Riemannian metric, no boundary, and its unique spin structure. Adopt the source-specific ABJM model on this background, viewed through one chosen three-dimensional $'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ subalgebra. Its gauge group is $G=U(N)'"\\times U(N)"'$ with $N'"\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}"'$ and supersymmetric Chern–Simons levels $k$ and $-k$, where $k'"\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}"'$. The global group includes both central $U(1)$ factors. Write $'"\\operatorname{tr}_N"'$ for the trace in the defining representation of either factor; in the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov convention the level multiplies $'"\\operatorname{tr}_N/(4\\pi)"'$ in the supersymmetric Chern–Simons action.

Each $'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ vector multiplet has a Hermitian matrix-valued connection $A_'"\\mu"'$ (or $'"\\widehat A_\\mu"'$), a real Hermitian adjoint scalar $'"\\sigma"'$ (or $'"\\widehat\\sigma"'$), a two-component complex gaugino $'"\\lambda"'$ (or $'"\\widehat\\lambda"'$), and a real auxiliary scalar $D$ (or $'"\\widehat D"'$). In an oriented coordinate chart the first factor'"'s source-normalized action is
"'$$
S_{'"\\mathrm{CS},k}=\\frac{k}{4\\pi}\\int d"'^3x'"\\,\\operatorname{tr}_N\\"'!'"\\left(\\epsilon"'^{'"\\mu\\nu\\rho}\\left(A_\\mu\\partial_\\nu A_\\rho+\\frac{2i}{3}A_\\mu A_\\nu A_\\rho\\right)-\\lambda"'^'"\\dagger\\lambda+2D\\sigma\\right);
"'$$
the second factor uses the same expression with $(A,'"\\lambda,D,\\sigma,k)"'$ replaced by $('"\\widehat A,\\widehat\\lambda,\\widehat D,\\widehat\\sigma,-k)"'$. Here $'"\\epsilon"'^{'"\\mu\\nu\\rho}"'$ is the oriented Levi-Civita density.

The matter consists of two chiral multiplets $A_a$, $a=1,2$, in $('"\\mathbf N,\\overline{\\mathbf N})"'$ and two $B_{'"\\dot a}"'$, $'"\\dot a=1,2"'$, in $('"\\overline{\\mathbf N},\\mathbf N)"'$. A chiral multiplet means a complex scalar, a two-component complex spinor, and a complex auxiliary scalar in its stated representation. For $A_a$, for example, a gauge transformation $(U,'"\\widehat U)\\in G"'$ acts by $A_a'"\\mapsto U A_a\\widehat U"'^{-1}$; the $B_{'"\\dot a}"'$ action is the reversed one. The covariant derivative on a bifundamental scalar is $D_'"\\mu A_a=\\partial_\\mu A_a+iA_\\mu A_a-iA_a\\widehat A_\\mu"'$; spinors also carry the Levi-Civita spin connection. The vector-multiplet fields act in that representation by left multiplication from the first factor minus right multiplication from the second.

The adopted $'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ superpotential is
$$
W='"\\frac{2\\pi}{k}\\epsilon"'^{ab}'"\\epsilon"'^{'"\\dot a\\dot b}\\operatorname{tr}_N(A_aB_{\\dot a}A_bB_{\\dot b})
=\\frac{4\\pi}{k}\\operatorname{tr}_N(A_1B_1A_2B_2-A_1B_2A_2B_1),
"'$$
where $'"\\epsilon"'^{12}=1=-'"\\epsilon"'^{21}$ and all other components vanish. This is the ABJM quartic interaction; the adopted quantum model has the enhanced $'"\\mathcal N=6"'$ superconformal symmetry, while localization uses only the selected $'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ subalgebra. The scalar component of each chiral multiplet has $U(1)_R$ charge and superconformal dimension $'"\\Delta=\\tfrac12"'$; its fermion and auxiliary component have charges $'"\\Delta-1"'$ and $'"\\Delta-2"'$. Set all real masses and Fayet–Iliopoulos parameters to zero. A real mass here is the constant scalar in a background flavor vector multiplet; a Fayet–Iliopoulos parameter is the coefficient of the auxiliary $D$ field for an abelian vector multiplet. The level $k$ and radius $r$ are dimensionless and length-valued, respectively; $[A_'"\\mu]=[\\sigma]=r"'^{-1}$ and the dimensionless Cartan variables used in the sourced matrix integral are $r$ times the constant scalar eigenvalues.

The smooth field domain consists of pairs of smooth connections and the listed smooth associated matter and auxiliary fields on the trivial $U(N)'"\\times U(N)"'$ bundles. The smooth gauge group $C''^'"\\infty(\\mathbb S"'^3_r,U(N)'"\\times U(N))"'$ acts by the transformations just specified. The source gauge-fixes this quotient in covariant (Lorenz) gauge with Faddeev–Popov ghosts. Its Euclidean contour takes the vector connections and real scalars on their Hermitian/real slices, pairs each matter scalar with its Hermitian conjugate, and integrates the fermions as independent Grassmann variables. Diagonalizing the constant Hermitian scalars leaves Cartan eigenvalues on real lines, with residual Weyl group $S_N'"\\times S_N"'$.

Choose a normalized conformal Killing spinor $'"\\epsilon"'$ satisfying $'"\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon=\\frac{i}{2r}\\gamma_\\mu\\epsilon"'$ in an orthonormal frame, where $'"\\gamma_\\mu"'$ are Hermitian two-dimensional gamma matrices. Let $Q$ be its off-shell supersymmetry transformation and let $'"\\delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}"'$ be the Faddeev–Popov differential. In the source gauge-fixed complex, $Q_{'"\\mathrm{gf}}=Q+\\delta_{\\mathrm{FP}}"'$ is odd and squares to zero on the gauge-fixed fields and ghosts. The source localization calculation adds its $Q_{'"\\mathrm{gf}}"'$-exact Yang–Mills deformation and evaluates the one-loop determinants with zeta-function regularization.

The corresponding gauge-fixed functional integral is only the formal expression
$$
Z_{'"\\mathrm{formal}}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal G)}\\int_{\\mathcal C/\\mathcal G}[D\\Phi]\\,e"'^{-S_E['"\\Phi]},
"'$$
where $'"\\mathcal G=C"'^'"\\infty(\\mathbb S"'^3_r,U(N)'"\\times U(N))"'$, $'"\\mathcal C"'$ is the source Euclidean contour, and $S_E$ is the supersymmetric Chern–Simons, chiral-matter, and superpotential action specified above. The source calculation assumes that its gauge-fixed measure and contour are $Q_{'"\\mathrm{gf}}"'$-invariant and that the deformation introduces no anomalous Jacobian or boundary term. These are adopted formal field-theory inputs: this postulate does not construct an infinite-dimensional measure, prove convergence of the localization limit, or assert a theorem for arbitrary three-dimensional $'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ models.

## Postulate references

[Kapustin, Willett, and Yaakov, §§2–4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4559)

[Mariño, §§2.1–2.4 and 6.2–6.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1104.0783)
