## Example

Fix the adopted zero-mass, zero-Fayet–Iliopoulos ABJM model on the round sphere of radius "'$r>0$, with $N'"\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}"'$ and nonzero integer level $k$ as in [[post-qftx-s3-cs-matter-localization-model]]. Let $s_i,'"\\widehat s_i\\in\\mathbb R"'$ be the eigenvalues of the two constant Hermitian Cartan scalars, each of physical dimension $r''^{-1}$, and put $'"\\lambda_i=rs_i"'$ and $'"\\widehat\\lambda_i=r\\widehat s_i"'$. In the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov Euclidean level convention, the source-reported formal localization expression is
$$
Z_{'"\\mathrm{ABJM}}(\\mathbb S"'^3_r)='"\\frac{1}{(N"'!)''^2}'"\\int_{\\mathbb R"'^{2N}}'"\\left(\\prod_{i=1}"'^N d'"\\lambda_i\\,d\\widehat\\lambda_i\\right)e"'^{-i'"\\pi k\\left(\\sum_i\\lambda_i"'^2-'"\\sum_i\\widehat\\lambda_i"'^2'"\\right)}
\\frac{\\displaystyle\\prod_{1\\le i<j\\le N}\\left(2\\sinh\\"'!'"\\left(\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\lambda_j)\\right)\\right)"'^2'"\\left(2\\sinh\\"'!'"\\left(\\pi(\\widehat\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j)\\right)\\right)"'^2}{'"\\displaystyle\\prod_{i,j=1}"'^N'"\\left(2\\cosh\\"'!'"\\left(\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j)\\right)\\right)"'^2}.
$$
Each Cartan variable is integrated over the real line. The integer level and the dimensionless eigenvalues fix every exponent and hyperbolic-function argument; the factor $(N!)''^{-2}$ is the inverse order of the two Weyl groups. Mariño'"'s convention uses "'$'"\\mu_i=2\\pi\\lambda_i"'$ and $g_s=2'"\\pi i/k_{\\mathrm{Marino}}"'$; it gives the same displayed phase after setting $k_{'"\\mathrm{Marino}}=-k"'$. The displayed real-contour expression is oscillatory. It is the source'"'s matrix-integral result under its formal localization and determinant prescriptions, not a construction of the continuum functional measure, a proof of the localization limit, or a formula for every three-dimensional "'$'"\\mathcal N=2"'$ theory.

## Example references

[Kapustin, Willett, and Yaakov, §§3–4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4559)

[Mariño, §§6.2–6.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1104.0783)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The source-specific ABJM field content, levels, radius, zero mass/FI parameters, selected supercharge, real Cartan contour, and formal measure qualifications of the Postulate.

[A1] [[post-qftx-s3-cs-matter-localization-model]]: the adopted model has global group $U(N)'"\\times U(N)"'$, levels $k,-k$, four chiral multiplets in two conjugate bifundamental pairs, and $'"\\Delta=R=\\tfrac12"'$. Its field-theory measure and localization argument are formal inputs, not a constructed continuum measure.

[F1] Kapustin, Willett, and Yaakov, §§3.1–3.5 and §4, give the gauge-fixed Cartan reduction, root/Vandermonde factor, one-loop determinants, and the ABJM real-line matrix-integral convention. The result is specific to their superconformal Chern–Simons–matter setup.

[F2] Mariño, §6.3 and Eq. (6.67), gives the zeta-regularized chiral determinant for canonical dimension $'"\\Delta=\\tfrac12"'$ in a self-conjugate representation. Its §6.5 Eq. (6.81) combines four bifundamental chirals into the ABJM denominator and states the opposite Euclidean level-phase convention.

## Proof

1.1 In the chosen Cartan, the positive roots of each $U(N)$ factor are $e_i-e_j$ for $i<j$. A bifundamental weight is $e_i-'"\\widehat e_j"'$ and its conjugate weight is $-(e_i-'"\\widehat e_j)"'$, so the corresponding scalar eigenvalues are $'"\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j"'$ and its negative. The radius rescaling $'"\\lambda_i=rs_i"'$ makes each variable dimensionless. [given, A1, F1, construct]

1.2 For a conjugate pair of chiral multiplets, [F2] assigns the product of the factors $(2'"\\cosh(\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j)))"'^{-1/2}$ and $(2'"\\cosh(-\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j)))"'^{-1/2}$ to each bifundamental weight pair. Since $'"\\cosh"'$ is even, this is $(2'"\\cosh(\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\widehat\\lambda_j)))"'^{-1}$. ABJM has two such conjugate pairs, giving the denominator power $-2$ for every $(i,j)$. [A1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 The adjoint determinant for the first $U(N)$ factor is the product over its roots; pairing the opposite roots gives $'"\\prod_{i<j}(2\\sinh(\\pi(\\lambda_i-\\lambda_j)))"'^2$, up to the source'"'s orientation-independent root sign. The second factor gives the analogous hatted product, and the two root signs cancel. Dividing by the residual Weyl groups "'$S_N$ and $S_N$ contributes $(N!)''^{-2}$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 Mariño'"'s dimensionless variables satisfy "'$'"\\mu_i=2\\pi\\lambda_i"'$ and $g_s=2'"\\pi i/k_{\\mathrm{Marino}}"'$. Therefore $-'"\\frac{1}{2g_s}\\sum_i(\\mu_i"'^2-'"\\widehat\\mu_i"'^2)=i'"\\pi k_{\\mathrm{Marino}}\\sum_i(\\lambda_i"'^2-'"\\widehat\\lambda_i"'^2)$. Setting $k_{'"\\mathrm{Marino}}=-k"'$ gives exactly the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov phase in the Example. The variables are integrated on the real Cartan lines; the phase is oscillatory. [F1, F2, algebra]

3.1 Multiplying the classical phase, the two vector determinants, the chiral determinant, the Cartan measures, and the Weyl quotient from steps 1.2–2.2 gives the displayed matrix integral. This reproduces the source-specific formula only under the adopted formal localization assumptions; it does not prove that the continuum field-space measure or localization limit exists. [given, A1, F1, F2, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎
